children.push(...Img("fig13_dec_idec_comparison.png", 560, 334, "Figure 13. Mean ARI (N = 80 seeds) per mechanism for the two deep-clustering baselines added above alongside the minimal and wide autoencoder rows from Table 5. DEC and IDEC recover mechanism A far more reliably than either autoencoder configuration, but mechanism B drops to zero and mechanism C remains at zero throughout.", 13));
children.push(P("DEC and IDEC make mechanism A's recovery substantially more reliable than either generic autoencoder configuration (55–59% of seeds above ARI 0.3, versus 18–20%), consistent with a purpose-built clustering loss doing a better job of shaping the latent space around the dominant, easiest-to-separate signal. But both purpose-built objectives make mechanism B's recovery worse, not better — 0 of 80 seeds for either DEC or IDEC, down from the generic autoencoder's unreliable but nonzero 18–49%. Mechanism C remains at 0% for every architecture and objective tested. Added capacity and a properly engineered clustering loss sharpen this paper's central collapse for the minority mechanisms rather than resolving it: a clustering-specific objective concentrates the latent representation on the single dominant structure at the expense of the harder-to-see ones, exactly the variance-budget competition argument of Section 3."));
children.push(H("5.4 Auto-detected diagnostic pipeline", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("To make the hand-informed pipeline less dependent on a human already knowing which columns correspond to which mechanism, we built a prototype detector (auto_diagnostic_pipeline.py) that scores every candidate feature pair on three cheap, label-free statistics: a distance-separability score (quick K-Means silhouette), an angular-minority-cluster score (DBSCAN in doubled-angle space, requiring the compact cluster to be a minority), and a density-bimodality score (BIC improvement of a 2- versus 1-component Gaussian mixture on log k-NN distance); and routes each mechanism-appropriate transform to whichever pair scores highest, above a pre-registered threshold."));
children.push(P("Evaluated across N = 80 seeds (1000–1079): the detector correctly identified the distance pair (feature_1, feature_2) in all 80 seeds and, when routed to K-Means, matched the hand-informed pipeline’s ARI closely (mean 0.967, SD 0.004, versus 0.965 hand-informed). For the relational pair, routing was correct in 48/80 seeds (60%); conditional on correct routing, mean ARI was 0.920 (SD 0.009), close to Section 5.5’s oracle ceiling, while the 32/80 seeds (40%) that routed to the wrong pair scored ARI approximately 0, giving an unconditional mean of 0.669 (SD 0.413). The density pair was never correctly identified in any of the 80 seeds: the density heuristic consistently preferred the relational pair over the true density pair. Automatic detection is fully reliable for distance-type structure, a coin-flip-like 60% for the relational mechanism with this bespoke heuristic, and not yet viable for the density mechanism. Section 5.5 reports a different, off-the-shelf statistic that does substantially better for the relational mechanism."));
children.push(H("5.5 Invariant Coordinate Selection as a kurtosis-based screen for the relational mechanism", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("Section 5.4’s angular heuristic is a bespoke statistic designed specifically for this benchmark’s ratio-line construction. We tested whether Invariant Coordinate Selection (ICS; Tyler, Critchley, Dümbgen and Oja, 2009), a pre-existing, general-purpose technique not built for this dataset, does better. ICS jointly diagonalizes the ordinary covariance matrix (COV) and the fourth-moment (kurtosis) scatter matrix COV4, which weights each centered observation by its squared Mahalanobis distance before forming a second covariance-like matrix. The resulting generalized eigenvalues equal 1, in population, along any direction where the data is exactly Gaussian, and depart from 1 along directions of excess or deficient kurtosis; ICS is affine-invariant, unlike PCA. A substantial literature establishes that ICS recovers group and outlier structure in elliptical mixture models via exactly this mechanism (Tyler et al., 2009; Alfons, Archimbaud, Nordhausen and Ruiz-Gazen, 2024), including a closed-form result for two-component Gaussian mixtures with shared covariance. Mechanism B’s relational subgroup is not an elliptical mixture component in that literature’s exact sense: it is a fixed-ratio line with radius drawn from Uniform(3,9), a heavy-tailed, non-Gaussian embedding, so the established closed-form theory does not directly apply; we treat our finding as an empirical extension of the qualitative ICS mechanism to this non-elliptical case, and flag a formal extension of the elliptical-mixture theory to ratio-line embeddings as future work (Section 9)."));
children.push(P("For every candidate feature pair, we compute the 2×2 ICS generalized eigenvalues of (COV4, COV) restricted to that pair, and take the eigenvalue farthest from 1 as a pair-level “kurtosis-anomaly score,” routed the same pre-registered-threshold way as Section 5.4 (the pair with the largest anomaly score is selected; no ground-truth labels are used for routing). We evaluate at seed 42 for comparability with Section 5.4, and across the same N = 80 seeds (1000–1079) for robustness."));
children.push(...Img("fig14_ics_kurtosis_screening.png", 560, 265, "Figure 14. Left: the ICS kurtosis-anomaly score at seed 42 for every candidate pair; the true relational pair (feature_3, feature_4) is a clear outlier (score 63.4, next-highest 10.7). Right: across 80 seeds, the true relational pair's score (gold) is consistently far above the true scale pair's score (gray, negative control).", 14));
children.push(P("Across all 80 seeds, ICS kurtosis screening selected the correct pair (feature_3, feature_4) 80/80 times (100%), versus Section 5.4’s DBSCAN-angular heuristic, evaluated on the identical 80 seeds, at 48/80 (60%). The mean kurtosis-anomaly score on the true relational pair was 63.29 (SD 1.06) versus 8.14 (SD 0.17) on the true scale pair used as a negative control — a gap of roughly 55 standard deviations of the negative control’s own variability."));
children.push(P("We re-ran the ICS routing procedure across the same 30-cell (relational_fraction, ratio) grid used in Section 4.3 (N = 15 seeds per cell, 450 runs total, disjoint seeds 5000–5014; ics_kurtosis_parameter_sweep.py). Routing accuracy is 100% (450/450) in every cell, including the weakest tested configuration (relational_fraction = 0.02, ratio = 1.2), where the margin between the true pair’s mean score (11.99) and the strongest competing pair’s (10.88) is far narrower than at the original configuration (63.25 versus 10.88) but still decisive across all 15 seeds. The oracle-ceiling ARI on the routed pair scales with signal strength, from a mean of 0.64 at the weakest tested cell to above 0.99 at the strongest, while routing accuracy does not degrade anywhere on the tested grid."));
children.push(...Img("fig15_ics_parameter_sweep.png", 560, 431, "Figure 15. ICS routing accuracy across the same 30-cell grid used to stress-test Theorem 1. Every cell is 100%; the blue box marks the paper's original default configuration.", 15));
children.push(P("Within-pair detection, reported at the same three tiers Section 6.1 uses for mechanism C. First, the oracle ceiling on this signal (best achievable ARI from any threshold on the squared projection onto the anomalous direction) is high and stable: mean 0.938 (SD 0.005) across 80 seeds, seed 42 = 0.944 (AUC = 0.999). Second, a fully unsupervised 2-component Gaussian-mixture threshold on the log-squared projection fails consistently: mean ARI -0.097 (SD 0.001), reflecting a GMM that converges to a near-median split rather than isolating the small, heavy-tailed component. Third, seeding the same GMM with a generic “a small minority exists” prior (10%, not the true 12%) does not materially help: mean ARI -0.074 (SD 0.031). ICS solves the routing half of the diagnostic problem for mechanism B essentially perfectly, and the underlying signal has an excellent, stable oracle ceiling, but converting that signal into an automatic, label-free membership prediction remains unsolved with the simple thresholding rules we tried — the same pattern already found for mechanism C’s density heuristic in Section 5.4."));
children.push(P("As a negative control, applying the identical ICS procedure to the true scale/density pair (feature_5, feature_6) gives a much weaker oracle ceiling, mean ARI 0.467 across 80 seeds, below both this section’s mechanism-B ceiling (0.938) and Section 5.2’s oracle-threshold ceiling for mechanism C (0.861, revised to 0.880 by the radial-centroid oracle in Section 5.6). This is expected: mechanism C’s hiding is a locally isotropic density contrast with no preferred linear direction, so no single linear projection, ICS included, can capture as much of it as a genuinely local, density-based statistic can. ICS is a strong, general-purpose, off-the-shelf routing tool specifically for kurtosis/heavy-tail-type (mechanism-B-like) hiding, not a universal fix."));
children.push(table(
  ["Method (routing/detection of the relational pair)", "Routing accuracy (N=80)", "Oracle ARI ceiling", "Best practical (label-free) ARI"],
  [
    ["DBSCAN-angular heuristic (Section 5.4)", "48/80 (60%)", "n/a", "0.920 (SD 0.009) if routed correctly; 0.669 (SD 0.413) unconditional"],
    ["ICS kurtosis screening (Section 5.5)", "80/80 (100%)", "0.938 (SD 0.005)", "-0.074 (generic-prior GMM; unsolved)"],
    ["ICS kurtosis screening + eigengap-routing + HDBSCAN (Section 6.6)", "80/80 (100%)", "0.938 (SD 0.005)", "0.831 (SD 0.059; eigengap-routed HDBSCAN, no fraction prior)"],
  ]
));
children.push(H("5.6 A simpler radial-distance-from-centroid baseline", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("To establish precisely what mechanisms B and C are and are not invisible to, we tested the simplest possible Euclidean-distance-based statistic we could construct: for each candidate 2-feature pair, the scalar Euclidean distance of every point from the pair’s global centroid, with no angle information, no local density or k-NN estimation, and no DBSCAN."));
children.push(P("Across the same N = 80 seeds used throughout Sections 5.4, 5.5 and 6 (radial_centroid_baseline.py), this single scalar separates the hidden group from the background almost perfectly for both mechanisms (AUC = 0.997, SD 0.0004 for mechanism B; AUC = 0.998, SD 0.0004 for mechanism C). An oracle threshold on this scalar achieves mean ARI = 0.875 (SD 0.009) for mechanism B — comparable to Section 5.2’s deployable hand-informed pipeline (0.915) — and mean ARI = 0.880 (SD 0.011) for mechanism C, which exceeds both Section 5.2’s k-NN-distance oracle ceiling (0.861) despite the two signals being constructed very differently, and Section 5.2’s deployable k-NN-density-GMM result (0.662), the figure that grounds Section 6.1’s headline collapse comparison. We revise Section 5.2’s oracle-threshold ceiling for mechanism C upward to 0.880; the deployable-pipeline figure (0.662) stands unchanged."));
children.push(P("We also re-ran plain K-Means (k = 2) directly on the raw, untransformed (feature_3, feature_4) coordinates across the same 80 seeds: it remains unreliable for mechanism B, mean ARI = 0.207 (SD = 0.257) — occasionally partial by chance on a favorable seed, never stable, and far below and far less consistent than the radius-threshold oracle. Mechanism B’s hidden points form two opposite rays (a line through the origin, not a compact blob), and mechanism C’s hidden points are a low-variance cloud sharing a centroid with a much more diffuse background, so neither has an off-center compact cluster for K-Means’ multi-centroid Voronoi partitioning to find. A single derived radial feature sidesteps this shape assumption entirely, without requiring K-Means at all. The failure documented throughout this paper is specific to K-Means’ partitioning strategy applied to non-blob-shaped structure, not to Euclidean distance as a general-purpose statistic; we revise the paper’s terminology throughout (abstract, Section 1.1, Section 4’s benchmark table) from “invisible to Euclidean distance” to the more precise “invisible to K-Means’ multi-centroid partitioning.”"));
children.push(P("This baseline sharpens the paper’s central claim: the naive PCA-preprocessed K-Means pipeline still collapses on mechanisms B and C (Section 6.1), and Silhouette still fails to flag it, but the precise reason is a geometric mismatch between K-Means’ cluster-shape assumption and the hidden structure’s shape (a line, or a shared-centroid density contrast), not an intrinsic invisibility to distance-based reasoning in general."));
children.push(H("6. Results", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(H("6.1 Headline collapse (seed 42)", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("With all three mechanisms present, the naive pipeline’s top-2-PC K-Means (k=4, Silhouette-selected) recovers the true 4-class structure at ARI = 0.115 (versus ARI = 0.965 for the same mechanism under the hand-informed pipeline), while Silhouette itself is 0.382, unremarkable and not flagged as anomalous by any conventional rule of thumb. The relational and scale mechanisms are recovered by the naive pipeline at ARI = 0.237 and ARI = 0.001 respectively (both far below their hand-informed references of 0.915 and 0.662, and below the radial-distance-from-centroid oracle ceilings of 0.875 and 0.880 reported in Section 5.6). The naive pipeline’s failure on mechanisms B and C is not explained by an absence of a strong Euclidean signal in the data — one exists, and a simple threshold on it recovers most of it — but by top-2-PC K-Means’ inability to find that signal via multi-centroid partitioning (Section 5.6)."));
