children.push(H("6.2 Multi-seed robustness (N = 80, up from N = 20)", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("We increased the multi-seed robustness check from 20 to 80 independent seeds (justified in Section 6.3’s power analysis). Across all 80 seeds, mean ARI for the distance mechanism was 0.059 (SD 0.035, range 0.003–0.148), while mean Silhouette was 0.379 (SD 0.006): the collapse and the accompanying false confidence both hold up, and the SD comparison (35% of the mean for ARI vs. 1.7% of the mean for Silhouette) sharpens the central claim — recovery quality is highly seed-dependent while the validator’s reported confidence is almost seed-independent."));
children.push(...Img("fig22_multiseed_robustness.png", 560, 373, "Figure 22. Distribution of ARI (variable) versus Silhouette (essentially constant) across 80 independent seeds.", 22));
children.push(H("6.3 Interaction hypothesis", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("We test whether combining mechanisms B and C damages recovery of mechanism A more than the sum of their individual, separately-measured damages (“super-additive” collapse). An N = 20 pilot design produced a positive interaction in 65% of seeds with paired Wilcoxon p = 0.23. A retrospective power analysis on the pilot’s N = 20 data (effect_size_power_stats.py) found ≈26% power at alpha = 0.05 to detect the pilot’s own observed paired effect size (Cohen’s d_z ≈ 0.33), with N ≈ 76 seeds needed for 80% power at that effect size — motivating the N = 80 replication reported below."));
children.push(P("We reran the full ablation at N = 80 seeds. The interaction effect, which had appeared positive in the pilot, shrank to essentially zero: mean interaction effect 0.0002 (compare 0.015 in the pilot), positive in 52.5% of seeds, paired Wilcoxon p = 0.85, and an equivalent OLS regression decomposition (ARI ~ B + C + B:C) gives an interaction-term p = 0.98."));
children.push(P("This null result is supported by a formal equivalence test: a two one-sided test (TOST) against a conventional small-effect boundary (Cohen’s d_z = 0.20, i.e. an interaction effect of 0.011 ARI units) rejects the presence of an interaction at least that large (p = 0.042), and the 95% confidence interval for the true mean interaction effect is [-0.012, 0.013], a narrow band centered on zero."));
children.push(P("To test whether this null result is specific to one benchmark configuration, we re-ran the identical ablation at three additional points on the same parameter grid used by Section 4.3 (relational_fraction/ratio = 0.04/1.6, 0.20/3.0, 0.30/4.0), each at N = 80 seeds with a disjoint seed stream. The null result holds at every point tested: mean interaction effect 0.007, -0.008, and 0.000 respectively, all with paired Wilcoxon p > 0.19. The interaction-effect sign is not even consistent across configurations (positive at the weak and extreme points, negative at the strong point), evidence against a real, systematically-directional interaction rather than noise. This covers four representative points on the grid, not the full 2D space."));
children.push(...Img("fig23_interaction_multiconfig.png", 560, 362, "Figure 23. Mean interaction effect (observed minus additive prediction) at four parameter-grid configurations (default, weak, strong, extreme; N=80 seeds each, error bars = 1 SD).", 23));
children.push(...Img("fig24_interaction_effect.png", 560, 362, "Figure 24. Distribution of the interaction effect across 80 seeds, centered at essentially zero.", 24));
children.push(P("The same regression decomposition finds a robust, statistically significant main effect of mechanism B on collapse of mechanism A’s recovery (coefficient -0.0223, p < 0.0001) and no significant main effect of mechanism C (coefficient +0.0016, p = 0.77). The relational mechanism (B) reliably makes the distance mechanism harder to recover on its own; the scale mechanism (C) does not, on average; and there is no good evidence that the two combine synergistically."));
