children.push(PR([{ text: "A complementary stress test: contamination breakdown points. ", bold: true }, { text: "The interaction test above asks whether mechanisms that are independent by construction behave as if independent; because dataset_lib.py draws each mechanism from its own RNG stream, a null result here has some foregone-conclusion character. A more demanding question is how much cross-mechanism dependence detection can tolerate before it fails (sensitivity_robustness.py). We contaminate all ten raw features with a shared per-sample latent factor g ~ N(0,1), X_c = √(1−c²)·X_raw + c·std_j·g, which preserves each feature’s own marginal variance exactly at every contamination strength c and induces an expected pairwise correlation of approximately c between every pair of features — corrupting within-block structure as well as cross-block structure, a stronger and more conservative stress test than corrupting only the cross-block correlations that Theorem 1’s ε formally bounds. At each of nine contamination levels (c = 0 to 0.8 in steps of 0.1) we re-run the paper’s hand-informed detectors and record each mechanism’s ARI, defining the breakdown point as the smallest tested c at which mean ARI first falls below half its c = 0 value." }]));
children.push(...Img("fig25_sensitivity_breakdown.png", 560, 402, "Figure 25. Mean ARI (+/- SD across 5 seeds) for each mechanism's hand-informed detector as cross-mechanism contamination strength c increases, with each mechanism's breakdown point marked.", 25));
children.push(P("The three mechanisms break down at markedly different contamination strengths (Figure 22, Table 3): mechanism A at c = 0.60, mechanism B at c = 0.40, and mechanism C at c = 0.10 — roughly six times more fragile than mechanism A on this measure. Mechanism C’s detector is already the weakest of the three at c = 0 and is the first to degrade as contamination increases, consistent with the density/scale mechanism being, throughout this paper, the hardest of the three to detect by any method. This result should be read as a lower-powered, complementary check rather than a replacement for the N = 80 interaction analysis above: it uses N = 5 seeds per contamination level, chosen to keep runtime manageable across nine contamination levels and three mechanisms simultaneously, and its breakdown-point estimates should be read as indicative of relative fragility ordering across mechanisms, not as precisely estimated thresholds."));
children.push(table(
  ["Mechanism", "Baseline ARI (c = 0)", "Breakdown point (c)", "ARI at breakdown"],
  [
    ["A (distance)", "0.967", "0.60", "0.404"],
    ["B (relational)", "0.914", "0.40", "0.407"],
    ["C (scale)", "0.671", "0.10", "0.010"],
  ]
));
children.push(H("6.4 Additional metrics and the Silhouette-ARI relationship", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("To check the collapse is not an ARI-specific artifact, we recomputed NMI and Purity alongside ARI at seed 42 across k = 2..6; all three move together (e.g., at the Silhouette-preferred k = 4: ARI = 0.115, NMI = 0.100, Purity = 0.374). We also tested whether Silhouette carries information about ARI across the 80 seeds at fixed k = 4. Computed with silhouette_score’s built-in sample_size=2000 subsampling (used for O(n²) speed), the Spearman correlation between Silhouette and ARI is rho = 0.16 (p = 0.15); this estimate proved unstable across different subsampling draws (rho ranging from 0.16 to 0.40 across 5 tested random states), an expected consequence of estimating silhouette from 25% of the 8,000 points. Using the full, non-subsampled Silhouette score instead, the Spearman correlation is rho = 0.54 (p = 2 × 10⁻⁷) and the Pearson correlation is r = 0.54 (p = 2 × 10⁻⁷) — moderate and highly significant. Silhouette therefore does carry real relative information about which seeds recover somewhat better than others, contrary to our earlier subsampled estimate. What it does not provide is usable absolute calibration: across the same 80 seeds, ARI never exceeds 0.148 (mean 0.059, SD 0.035) while Silhouette remains essentially flat and confident-looking throughout (mean 0.379, SD 0.004). A researcher could use Silhouette to correctly guess, on average, which of two seeds recovered somewhat better; the same researcher would have no way to infer from any single Silhouette value in this range that true recovery is this poor in absolute terms."));
children.push(H("6.5 Summary view: which methods see which mechanism", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("Sections 5–6 report roughly thirty individual ARI numbers across six methods and three mechanisms; Figure 26 collects all of them into a single view. The entire top-left block (naive and sparse PCA, both mechanisms A and B) is a uniform, saturated red, confirming that linear variance-based reduction is close to uninformative for both of those recovery targets simultaneously. No single row is green across all three columns, including the hand-informed row, which is the best available result for each mechanism individually but still only reaches 0.662 for mechanism C. The radial-centroid row (oracle ARI 0.875 for mechanism B, 0.880 for mechanism C, Section 5.6) sits alongside the hand-informed and ICS rows as a third near-best-available result, without changing this pattern: no informed method reaches a uniformly green row across all three mechanisms."));
children.push(...Img("fig26_detection_matrix_summary.png", 560, 479, "Figure 26. Method x mechanism recovery matrix. The ICS row (Section 5.3) reports its oracle ceiling, not an automatic end-to-end result.", 26));
children.push(H("6.6 Automatic Within-Pair Detection Revisited: Eigengap-Based Routing and HDBSCAN", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
children.push(P("Section 5.3 left one thread visibly loose: Invariant Coordinate Selection routes to the correct feature pair with perfect reliability, yet turning that routing signal into an automatic, label-free membership call defeated every Gaussian-mixture strategy we tried — an unsupervised 2-component GMM on the log-squared projection (mean ARI -0.097) and the same GMM seeded with a generic 10% minority-weight prior (mean ARI -0.074) both collapsed to a near-median split, missing the small, heavy-tailed relational subgroup outright rather than merely approximating it. We closed this loop by removing, rather than relaxing, the two manual assumptions built into that pipeline. Routing confidence is now decided automatically: an eigengap rule applied to the sorted seven-pair kurtosis-deviation spectrum from Section 5.3 requires the largest gap between consecutive scores to fall immediately after the top-ranked pair — the same largest-gap logic spectral clustering uses to pick a number of clusters, repurposed here to certify the routing decision itself. And the fixed 2-component GMM is retired in favor of HDBSCAN (Campello, Moulavi and Sander, 2013), applied to the identical log-squared-projection space the GMM used, but left free to discover its own number of clusters and its own noise class, with its one sensitivity knob (min_cluster_size) fixed at a generic 3% of n rather than the true 12% relational fraction the GMM implicitly assumed (code/ICS_Eigengap_HDBSCAN.py)."));
children.push(P("The change is decisive. At seed 42 — the same seed anchoring Figure 14 — kurtosis-deviation routing again locks onto (feature_3, feature_4) with a score of 63.4 against a next-highest of 10.7, and an eigengap ratio of 0.83 comfortably clears the confidence threshold; HDBSCAN then carves two clusters out of the log-squared projection and reaches ARI = 0.836 against the hidden relational label, just short of that seed's 0.944 oracle ceiling. The pattern holds well beyond one lucky seed: across the same N = 80 seeds used throughout Sections 5–6, routing is correct and confident on all 80, and mean ARI reaches 0.831 (SD 0.059) against an oracle ceiling of 0.938 (SD 0.005) — recovering most of the achievable signal and turning the GMM's near-zero-or-negative ARI into something a practitioner could plausibly deploy. The gain is not a free lunch in disguise: run on the true scale/density pair (feature_5, feature_6) as a negative control, the identical procedure manages only ARI 0.072 against the hidden scale label, confirming that what eigengap-plus-HDBSCAN fixes is specific to kurtosis/heavy-tail structure, not a general-purpose patch for mechanism C — exactly the boundary already drawn for ICS routing itself in Section 5.3."));
children.push(...Img("fig27_ics_eigengap_hdbscan.png", 560, 233, "Figure 27. Eigengap-routed, HDBSCAN-clustered minority recovery across N = 80 seeds. Left: the distribution of practical ARI against the hidden relational label (green) sits just below, and closely tracks the shape of, the oracle ceiling (grey). Right: eigengap ratio (routing confidence) against the resulting ARI — every one of the 80 seeds routes correctly, and confidence stays in a narrow, stable band regardless of how well the downstream clustering does.", 27));
children.push(P("Together these two changes narrow, without closing, the automatic-detection gap the ICS row of Figure 26 leaves visible by reporting only an oracle ceiling. Density-based clustering with automatic model selection turns most — not all — of ICS's routing success into something that runs without a human in the loop and without smuggling in the answer through a hand-set minority fraction; the residual gap between HDBSCAN's practical ARI (0.831) and the oracle ceiling (0.938) is small enough to be encouraging and large enough to remain an open target for future thresholding refinements (Section 9)."));
children.push(H("6.7 Consolidated Final Numbers", HeadingLevel.HEADING_2));
