children.push(H("7. Discussion", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(P("The structural mechanism for why linear PCA spreads variance near-uniformly under multi-mechanism co-presence holds (Proposition 1, verified numerically). This near-uniform spread, and the resulting collapse of even the easiest mechanism’s recovery under linear PCA, is robust across dimensionality and seeds, and worsens rather than improves with realistic added dimensionality. Silhouette gives no reliable signal of this collapse, remaining both high-confidence-looking and, at best, weakly informative about actual recovery quality. The density/scale mechanism specifically remains invisible to every generic, unsupervised front end tried — linear, nonlinear, kurtosis-based, or deep."));
children.push(P("The relational mechanism is not uniformly invisible to every generic method. Kernel PCA and t-SNE do not see it (Section 5.3), our own DBSCAN-angular heuristic does not reliably route to it (Section 5.4), and a deep autoencoder baseline recovers it only unreliably, in a minority of random-initialization seeds and never together with mechanism A (Section 5.3) — but Invariant Coordinate Selection, a 15-year-old, off-the-shelf, general-purpose statistical technique not built for this dataset, routes to the correct pair essentially perfectly (100% across 80 seeds) via a kurtosis-anomaly signal with a stable, high oracle ceiling (0.938). The residual gap is specifically in automatic within-pair thresholding, not in locating which pair matters."));
children.push(P("Mechanisms B and C do not interact super-additively in their damage to mechanism A’s recovery (Section 6.3, N = 80, TOST-confirmed). Linear PCA’s collapse of the distance mechanism specifically is not a fundamental limit: Kernel PCA and t-SNE recover it well. The failure mode is not a general property of naive PCA+K-Means+Silhouette on arbitrary real tabular data: it was not observed on two of the three real datasets tested here. And the relational mechanism is not invisible to every generic unsupervised front end: ICS’s routing accuracy (Section 5.5) and, less reliably, a deep-clustering baseline’s bimodal partial recovery (Section 5.3) both recover it under specific conditions, even though neither fully solves automatic detection end-to-end."));
children.push(P("Practical implication: for datasets suspected of containing multiple, independent, low-linear-correlation sources of structure, we recommend (a) inspecting the raw PCA eigenvalue spectrum for near-uniformity as a cheap red flag (Section 3), (b) not treating a stable, moderate Silhouette score as evidence of good recovery without an independent check, (c) trying at least one nonlinear reduction method (Kernel PCA or t-SNE) before concluding a distance-type mechanism is undiscoverable, and (d) computing pairwise ICS kurtosis-anomaly scores as a cheap, general-purpose screen for ratio/relational-type hiding specifically, while recognizing that a correctly routed pair may still require further mechanism-specific work to turn into an automatic membership prediction, and that density/scale-defined subgroups remain invisible to all of the above."));
children.push(H("8. Limitations", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(Bullet("Theorem 1 is a proved, general, distribution-free worst-case bound (Weyl + Gershgorin), but it is a bound given a small cross-mechanism correlation ε, not a proof that ε is small for arbitrary generative processes. Theorem 2 partially closes this gap under a population-independence assumption, improving the resulting dimension dependence from O(d) to O(√d); it still assumes approximate population independence and does not track its constants explicitly."));
children.push(Bullet("Theorem 3 bounds eigenvector, not only eigenvalue, stability, but only while the relevant eigengap stays bounded away from zero; Proposition 2 shows this guarantee degrades toward total non-identifiability as a hypothetical benchmark approaches full isotropy, a regime this paper’s actual benchmark stays far from."));
children.push(Bullet("The benchmark, and all scaling/noise experiments, remain entirely synthetic; the real-data check (Section 4.2) uses only 3 datasets, none domain-matched (banking, clinical, etc.) or constructed to contain the specific three mechanisms studied here."));
children.push(Bullet("The re-powered N = 80 interaction analysis was extended to three additional parameter-grid configurations beyond the original default (Section 4.3); the null result holds at all four points tested but does not cover the full 2D grid."));
children.push(Bullet("The automatic mechanism-detection prototypes (Sections 5.4–5.5) together solve at most 2 of 3 mechanism types, and only partially: distance-type routing and clustering both succeed; relational-type routing succeeds via ICS but automatic within-pair thresholding does not; density-type detection does not succeed at either stage. All detectors were tuned informally against this benchmark’s known mechanisms in the absence of an independent labeled calibration corpus and should be read as proof-of-concept diagnostics, not validated detectors."));
children.push(Bullet("Section 5.5’s ICS finding is an empirical extension of established elliptical-mixture ICS theory (Tyler et al., 2009) to a non-elliptical, ratio-line embedding; we verify the qualitative mechanism numerically across 80 seeds but do not supply a new closed-form theorem for the non-elliptical case."));
children.push(Bullet("A deep-clustering (autoencoder + K-Means) baseline was added (Section 5.3), including a wider architecture, which did not resolve and by some measures worsened the seed-dependent instability, ruling out architecture capacity alone as the explanation. A comparison against a purpose-built deep-clustering method (e.g. DEC/IDEC) was not attempted."));
children.push(Bullet("Section 5.6’s radial-distance-from-centroid baseline shows a simple Euclidean scalar recovers both mechanisms B and C at oracle ARI comparable to or above the hand-informed pipeline’s reference ceilings. The collapse is specific to K-Means’ multi-centroid Voronoi partitioning applied to non-blob-shaped structure, not to Euclidean distance as a general-purpose statistic; the naive PCA+K-Means+Silhouette collapse itself (Section 6.1) and Theorem 1 are unaffected by this distinction. We recommend any future citation of this paper’s “invisibility” claim use the K-Means-specific formulation."));
children.push(Bullet("The blind clustering-step comparison (Section 5.3) and the contamination sensitivity analysis (Section 6.3) both grid-search or otherwise favor their methods against ground-truth ARI, an oracle/best-case comparison; neither claims these settings would be found automatically in a fully label-free deployment, a gap shared with every other automatic-detection result in this paper (Sections 5.4–5.5)."));
children.push(Bullet("The contamination breakdown-point analysis (Section 6.3) uses N = 5 seeds per contamination level, an order of magnitude fewer than the N = 80 standard used elsewhere in this paper for multi-seed robustness; its breakdown points should be read as an approximate fragility ordering across mechanisms, not as precisely estimated thresholds."));
children.push(H("9. Future Work", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(Bullet("Removing Theorem 2’s population-independence assumption entirely, tracking its constants explicitly, and extending it beyond sub-Gaussian features to broader classes of generative processes, to upgrade it from a first-principles bound conditional on independence to a full assumption-free asymptotic characterization."));
children.push(Bullet("Domain-matched real-data validation is now reported for network-traffic data (Section 4.2, CIC-IDS2017); the same real-data validation on banking, clinical, or other tabular domains with plausible analogous relational/ratio or density/scale subgroups remains open, as does a jointly-occurring (rather than per-mechanism-independent) real-data replication of Section 6.3's interaction study."));
children.push(Bullet("Closing the routing/thresholding gap identified in Section 5.5: a principled (not simple-GMM) automatic threshold for the ICS anomalous-direction signal, e.g. via extreme-value theory for the tail of the projected distribution, or a learned threshold calibrated across many synthetic instances of the relational mechanism at varying minority fractions and ratios."));
children.push(Bullet("A formal extension of Tyler et al.’s (2009) elliptical-mixture ICS eigenvalue theory to non-elliptical, ratio-line (or more generally, algebraically-constrained) embeddings, to replace Section 5.5’s empirical verification with a closed-form guarantee analogous to Theorem 1."));
children.push(Bullet("Section 5.6’s radial-distance-from-centroid statistic was tested only as an oracle-threshold diagnostic, on the correct feature pair (routing assumed, not solved). Two extensions are open: (a) an automatic, label-free threshold on the radial statistic itself; and (b) testing whether augmenting the naive pipeline’s feature set with per-pair radial-distance features, computed automatically for every candidate pair, allows PCA+K-Means to partially self-rescue without any hand-informed transform."));
children.push(Bullet("Improving the density-mechanism heuristic (Section 5.4), which neither the DBSCAN-angular nor the ICS approach detects well, e.g. via a learned or better-calibrated local-density statistic."));
children.push(Bullet("Extending the benchmark to categorical/mixed-type features, missing data, and heavy-tailed (non-Gaussian) mechanisms."));
children.push(Bullet("A full UMAP (McInnes et al., 2018) comparison and a systematic sweep over reduction-method hyperparameters (perplexity, kernel bandwidth) rather than the single default configuration used here."));
children.push(Bullet("A purpose-built deep-clustering method (e.g. DEC/IDEC), since both a minimal and a substantially wider autoencoder architecture showed the same bimodal instability in Section 5.3, ruling out bottleneck capacity as the explanation and pointing instead to a more fundamental property of the autoencoder+K-Means approach on this benchmark."));
children.push(H("10. Conclusion", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(P("This paper combines a verified structural account of the phenomenon it documents (Theorem 1 and Theorem 2, Section 3) with tests of that account’s robustness across dimensionality, noise scale, alternative reduction methods, and real data, and a rigorously powered statistical treatment of its most quantitative claim: mechanisms B and C do not interact super-additively in their damage to mechanism A’s recovery (Section 6.3, N = 80, TOST-confirmed). We further tested whether a classical, general-purpose statistical technique outside our own construction (Invariant Coordinate Selection) could do what a bespoke heuristic could not: it routes near-perfectly to the correct relational-mechanism pair, though automatic within-pair thresholding remains open. We also tested a substantially wider autoencoder against the deep-clustering baseline’s bimodal instability, and found that widening reproduces the same instability rather than resolving it, ruling out architecture size as the explanation."));
children.push(P("The paper’s contribution is a small, precisely mechanism-specific set of claims: a verified structural reason for near-uniform PCA spectra under mechanism co-presence, a robust and worsening-with-dimensionality collapse of naive recovery accompanied by flat Silhouette confidence, and a precise statement of which kinds of hidden structure remain invisible to generic unsupervised pipelines, and which do not, once the right off-the-shelf statistical tool is applied. Section 5.6 establishes the exact scope of the central “invisibility” claim: mechanisms B and C are not invisible to Euclidean distance in general, only to K-Means’ multi-centroid partitioning of it, since a single radial-distance-from-centroid scalar recovers both at oracle ARI levels matching or exceeding the hand-informed reference pipeline. The headline empirical finding is unchanged by this precision: the naive PCA-preprocessed K-Means pipeline still collapses on these mechanisms while Silhouette stays falsely confident, and the precision sharpens the causal story for why."));
children.push(H("Appendix A: Reproducibility Manifest", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
