children.push(P("The CIC-IDS2017 domain-matched validation (Section 4.2) derives from the original raw archive (Sharafaldin, Lashkari and Ghorbani, 2018; Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity, University of New Brunswick), which is not redistributed in this package for size reasons. To reproduce 01_extract_pool.py, download the eight raw MachineLearningCVE CSV files from the official CIC-IDS2017 distribution, place them under cicids2017/raw/, and note in your own run log which processing-day files and dataset version/checksum you used, since a revised community release with minor value differences also circulates. All downstream files (build, screening, and figure-generation scripts; the three derived, already-labeled test CSVs; and the raw per-resample results) are included directly and do not require re-downloading anything to verify Section 4.2's reported numbers."));
children.push(H("Appendix B: Full Derivation of Theorem 2", HeadingLevel.HEADING_1));
children.push(P("This appendix gives the complete derivation of Theorem 2, the matrix-Bernstein-based tightening of Corollary 1 stated in Section 3."));
children.push(P("Corollary 1 bounds the empirical cross-block noise by concentrating each entry of the sample correlation matrix separately and combining entries via a union bound through Theorem 1’s Gershgorin row-sum route, costing a factor of (d − 1) in the final operator-norm bound: ‖Ê‖₂ = O(d·√(log(d)/n)) once combined with Theorem 1. A direct matrix-concentration argument avoids this loss and removes the union bound entirely."));
children.push(P("Let x₁,…,xₙ be n i.i.d. copies of a standardized, sub-Gaussian random vector in R^d with sub-Gaussian norm at most K, and suppose the population correlation matrix is exactly block-diagonal (R = R_B), as holds here because the mechanisms are generated from independent random streams. Let Ê be the empirical cross-block matrix (Ê_ij = the sample correlation between columns i and j for cross-block pairs, 0 otherwise, including the diagonal), so that:"));
children.push(P("Ê = (1/n) Σₖ Sₖ (9)"));
children.push(P("where Sₖ is the d × d symmetric matrix with (Sₖ)_ij = xₖ(i)xₖ(j) for cross-block pairs and 0 elsewhere. Because population cross-block correlations are exactly zero under mechanism independence, E[Sₖ] = 0, so the Sₖ are i.i.d., mean-zero, symmetric random matrices and Ê is their average."));
children.push(P("Bounding ‖Sₖ‖₂ by an entrywise tail bound and a union bound over its d(d − 1) cross-block entries correctly bounds any single entry but not the operator norm of the whole matrix, because entries in a common row of Sₖ share the common factor xₖ(i), so Sₖ is dominated by the rank-one term xₖxₖᵀ rather than behaving like a matrix of independent entries. We instead decompose Sₖ = xₖxₖᵀ − Dₖ, where Dₖ is xₖxₖᵀ restricted to the within-mechanism blocks and the diagonal, bounding the two pieces separately: ‖Sₖ‖₂ ≤ ‖xₖ‖₂² + ‖Dₖ‖₂. The first term is O(d) with probability 1 − δ/n, uniformly over k = 1,…,n, by the Hanson-Wright concentration inequality applied to ‖xₖ‖₂² (Rudelson and Vershynin, 2013) with a union bound over the n samples. The second term is O(K²log(dn)) by the same entrywise truncation argument, valid here because it applies to the small, fixed-size within-block part rather than the full cross-block matrix. For every dimensionality tested here (d = 10 to 100 against n = 8,000), d dominates this log factor, so, for an absolute constant c₁:"));
children.push(P("‖Sₖ‖₂ ≤ R₀ := c₁K²d (10)"));
children.push(P("For the matrix variance proxy σ²: for i ≠ l, E[(Sₖ²)ᵢₗ] = E[xₖ(i)xₖ(l)]·(…) = 0, since coordinates i and l are independent and mean-zero, whether or not they share a block; only diagonal entries survive, with E[(Sₖ²)ᵢᵢ] = d − bᵢ ≤ d, where bᵢ is the size of i’s own block. So E[Sₖ²] is diagonal with entries at most d, giving, for an absolute constant c₂:"));
children.push(P("σ² := ‖Σₖ E[Sₖ²]‖₂ ≤ c₂·n·(d − 1)·K⁴ (11)"));
children.push(P("Applying the matrix Bernstein inequality (Tropp, 2015, Thm. 6.1.1) to Σₖ Sₖ gives, for any t > 0:"));
